WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:09.250
The current Ebola virus outbreak in West Africa
is the largest and most wide spread in History,

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.620
with over 21,000 known cases to date and counting.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:22.029
Let's reveal some surprising facts about this
deadly virus.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:26.290
1.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.590
The first known instance of Ebola was in 1976.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:37.430
It appeared in Zaire in the Democratic Republic
of the Congo and killed 280 people.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.460
2.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.370
But the 1976 case may not be the first outbreak.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:50.120
A study on infectious diseases suggested the
first outbreak of Ebola may have occurred

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.400
around 430 B.C. in ancient Greece.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:01.510
The writings of Thucydides, described a virulent
disease that wiped out half of the city.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:08.420
His description of the disease's symptoms
is very similar to Ebola, including uncontrollable

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.420
bleeding.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.420
3.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.720
Ebola is named after the Ebola River in the
Democratic Republic of the Congo, which is

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.890
60 miles away from the first ever case of
the virus.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.890
4.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:32.760
In the current outbreak a handful of West
African countries have been infected, and

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.760
9 non-African countries.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.280
Spain, Germany, France, Switzerland, Netherlands,
Norway, Italy, the UK and the USA.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.280
5.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.690
Past Ebola outbreaks have had fatality rates
of up to 90%.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:48.229
But the fatality rate of the current outbreak
in West Africa it 50%.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:52.780
However outside of Africa, the fatality rate
is much lower.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:59.490
The current fatality rate in the United States
and Europe is a significantly lower 20%.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.450
Proving that where you're treated makes an
enormous difference.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.450
6.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:10.500
Ebola is not airborne and therefore cannot,
in its current form, be transmitted through

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.860
the air, via coughing or sneezing.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.709
It also can't be transmitted through water.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:22.069
The virus can only be transmitted through
direct contact with the blood or body fluids

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:24.290
of an Ebola sufferer.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:30.629
This makes Ebola surprisingly difficult to
spread, when compared to more contagious diseases

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:33.319
such as Measles or Influenza.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.319
7.

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:39.549
Because of this many disease specialists have
said that Ebola poses no threat to wealthier

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:46.069
countries in Europe and North America because
of their higher hygiene standards and better

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:48.409
disease containment capabilities.

00:02:48.409 --> 00:02:49.409
8.

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:56.780
2014 is not the first time in history that
Ebola has appeared in the United States.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:04.499
In 1989, macaque monkeys in the primate unit
of Hazelton Research Product's lab in Reston,

00:03:04.499 --> 00:03:09.260
Virginia, began dying with Ebola like symptoms.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:15.810
Four workers were exposed to the virus and
the military and CDC were called in to eliminate

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.480
the monkeys and quarantine the humans.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:26.569
But strangely it appeared to be a new strain
of Ebola that was only lethal to primates.

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:33.919
The new strain did however display a frightening
ability to go airborne for the first time.

00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:34.919
9.

00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:43.629
There is currently no tried and tested vaccine
for Ebola but in September 2014, a clinical

00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:50.059
trial of a new potential Ebola vaccine was
able to protect monkeys against the virus

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:53.389
in 100% of cases.

00:03:53.389 --> 00:04:00.069
The same vaccine has just begun a trial at
Oxford University on humans, for the first

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:01.360
time ever.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.639
If the trials are successful the company that
manufactures the virus says it could have

00:04:07.639 --> 00:04:11.810
2 million doses of the vaccine available this
year.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:12.919
10.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:18.660
Several potential cures for the Ebola virus
are also currently being developed and tested.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.400
One of the most promising is called ZMapp.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.820
It was given to seven Ebola patients, two
of whom died.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.700
Showing a reasonable success rate.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.160
But the drug is difficult and time consuming
to make.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.220
And the limited supply has now been exhausted.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.210
It will take several months to manufacture
a new batch.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:47.170
However seven other potential Ebola cures
are currently undergoing clinical trials.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:48.170
11.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.180
There are five known strains of the Ebola
virus.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:58.570
These strains are called Zaire, Sudan, Taï
Forest, Reston and Bundibugyo.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:01.970
Named after the locations of each strain's
patient zero.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:08.600
A person who survives an Ebola infection is
then immune to that particular strain but

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.710
is still susceptible to the other four strains.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.710
12.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:18.980
One of the biggest challenges facing the containment
of the Ebola outbreak is misinformation and

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.850
distrust of health professionals in West Africa.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:29.650
Many sufferers of Ebola don't seek medical
care as they fear going into isolation and

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.650
being around other ebola patients.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:39.000
This vastly increases the chance of the individual
transmitting the virus to friends and family.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.740
There is also a tradition in West Africa of
washing dead bodies before burial.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:50.080
This has vastly increased the rate of infection
and is a significant issue in the current

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.830
outbreak.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:57.180
Because Ebola can live on a patient's skin
for several days after death.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.500
Meaning you could get infected from simply
touching the corpse of an Ebola victim then

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.470
touching your mouth or eyes.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.470
13.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:11.960
It is still not known where Ebola actually
comes from, but evidence suggests that fruit

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.530
bats are the natural carriers of the disease.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:21.210
Research suggests that the patient zero of
the current outbreak was a 2-year-old in Guinea,

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:27.300
who had recently been playing in a bat tree
when he contracted the disease.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.770
Known as bushmeat, bats and other wild animals
are frequently eaten by natives of Guinea

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:40.930
and bat soup is a delicacy in the region,
which could also have contributed to the start

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:46.720
of the current Ebola outbreak.

